    description: "**Defect.** The language-composition summary presents the repository as an 18-language polyglot, where most of the extra languages come only from test fixtures.\n\n**Evidence.** `profile.languages` reports 18 languages including go/rust/java/csharp/solidity/wgsl/swift, each ~{files:1, loc:~20}, all sourced from tests/fixtures/schema-coverage-corpus/<lang>/. Most of those fixture nodes are flagged is_test_file=True (csharp 6/6, wgsl 5/5, go 7/9, rust 6/9, java 5/6, solidity 7/8, swift 6/7). The application is a Python+TypeScript project. The profile counts every file the analyzer enumerates with no exclusion of test-fixture files.\n\n**Contract.** A \"what is this written in?\" composition summary should reflect the real codebase composition, or document that fixture languages are included. (Note: spec §profile defines languages[L].files as \"count the analyzer would enumerate\" with no test exclusion, so this is per-contract — weak/borderline.)\n\n**Consequence.** A consumer asking what languages the project uses is told it is a 7-extra-language polyglot (incl. Solidity/WGSL/Swift) when those appear only as test fixtures — a misleading picture of the technology stack.\n\n**Related (not duplicate).** Adjacent to INV-nohod (test-dominated node count) but a distinct surface: the codebase-composition summary."  # 640f
    fields: {}  # 640f
